er III</li> <li>Chapter IV</li> <li>Chapter V</li> <li>Chapter VI</li> </ul> <p>Back matter</p> <ul> <li>Epilogue</li> </ul>"},{"location":"philosophy-history-classics/consolation-of-philosophy-boethius/index.html#about-this-book","title":"About this book","text":"<p>Imprisoned and awaiting execution, Boethius is visited by Lady Philosophy and argues his way from fortune and loss to the nature of happiness, providence and freedom.</p> <p>From Standard Ebooks · CC0 1.0</p><p>Boethius (about 477–524), De consolatione philosophiae (Latin), about 524, translated by Henry Rosher James (1862–1931), 1897. This book is not stored in this library: each chapter is read from the public repository of its Standard Ebooks edition when it is opened, shown in this library's format, and ends with its own attribution. Standard Ebooks edition, CC0 1.0 (public domain dedication); transcription originally from Project Gutenberg. Modified: converted to this format.</p>"},{"location":"philosophy-history-classics/consolation-of-philosophy-boethius/book-1.html","title":"Chapter 1: Song I: Boethius’ Complaint","text":""},{"location":"philosophy-history-classics/consolation-of-philosophy-boethius/chapter-1-1.html","title":"Chapter I","text":""},{"location":"philosophy-history-classics/consolation-of-philosophy-boethius/chapter-1-1.html#21-song-ii-his-despondency","title":"2.1 Song II: His Despondency","text":""},{"location":"philosophy-history-classics/consolation-of-philosophy-boethius/chapter-1-2.html","title":"Chapter II","text":""},{"location":"philosophy-history-classics/consolation-of-philosophy-boethius/chapter-1-2.html#31-song-iii-the-mists-dispelled","title":"3.1 Song III: The Mists Dispelled","text":""},{"location":"philosophy-history-classics/consolation-of-philosophy-boethius/chapter-1-3.html","title":"Chapter III","text":""},{"location":"philosophy-history-classics/consolation-of-philosophy-boethius/chapter-1-3.html#41-song-iv-nothing-can-subdue-virtue","title":"4.1 Song IV: Noth